WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Voters in the United Kingdom have voted
to leave the European Union.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.550
"I hope this victory brings down this failed project and leads us to a Europe of sovereign nation state"

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.320
It’s a major blow to European political and economic integration, and a

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.100
huge embarrassment for Prime Minister David Cameron, who might lose

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.660
his job as a result. *Cameron just announced he will resign

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.890
"Britain is better off inside the EU than out on our own.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:32.550
At the heart of that is the single market.  500 million customers on our

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:38.790
doorstep. A source of so many jobs, so much trade, a such a wealth of opportunity

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:39.989
for our young people."

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.160
Britain’s exit will also have huge implications for

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.379
the British economy. 
The EU functions as a single integrated economy kind of like

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:53.250
the United States. And now that Britain
is out regulations could make it harder

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.770
to move goods across the English Channel.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.579
Lots of multinational corporations have
their headquarters in London, but now

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:03.210
they might find that it makes more sense
to have their European headquarters in

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:08.640
the European Union, which could lead to
job losses in Britain. The EU also allows

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.510
for the free movement of people within
its member states, which means that any

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.950
EU citizen can live and work in any
other EU country without a visa. And the

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.700
UK high employment rate has drawn
migrants from poorer countries in the EU

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.409
like Poland and Lithuania. But many in
Britain resent these migrants, and that

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:29.220
resentment was a key driving force in
the decision of for Britain to leave the

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.450
European Union.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.960
So now that Britain's out,  nobody really
knows what's going to happen to all

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.460
those EU migrants.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.920
So what comes next? A long negotiation
between the UK

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.150
and the EU member states where they have
to hash out issues like trade tariffs

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.509
agriculture immigration and a whole host
of other issues.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:51.380
Nobody really knows what this means for
the other EU countries – they could rally

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:55.790
together and push for even more
integration, or some countries could look

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.539
at Britain and say we should get out too.
But one thing is clear:

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:02.780
Britain's exit is a huge blow to the
dream of a united Europe.

